{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>For the leader. May YHWH answer you on the day of trouble.</b> It is possible that one of the poets composed this psalm about David, and the meaning of לדוד is 'about David,' like לשלמה in 'Elohim, your judgments to the king give' (Psalms 72:1), and do not wonder about the lamed in לדוד. Now there is another way: 'A psalm, a song for the Sabbath day' — or David composed it and says to his prince, 'May YHWH answer you on the day of trouble,' or he speaks about himself. And some say: it is about the Messiah." "<b>May YHWH answer you on the day of trouble</b> — in war." "And the meaning of <b>the Elohim of Jacob</b> — because many troubles befell him on the roads, and HaShem fortified him."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The phrase 'A psalm, a song for the Sabbath day' (Psalms 92:1) appears as an analogy for a superscription specifying an occasion; Ibn Ezra then pivots to the view that David himself composed Psalm 20."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:28:05.355900945Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}